.\" pcapsh(1) \- interactive packet shell and scripting language
.\" Part of libpcapng.  View with: man ./pcapsh.1
.TH PCAPSH 1 2026-10-13 "libpcapng" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pcapsh \- build, dissect and write network packets interactively or from scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcapsh
.RB [ \-p
.IR file.posa ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR keylog ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR out.pcapng ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ file.posa ...]
.RI [ script.pcapsh ]
.br
.B pcapsh
.RI [ options ]
.B \-e
.I expression
.br
.B pcapsh
.BR \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pcapsh
is a Scapy\-inspired shell built on libpcapng.
Packets are written as stacks of protocol layers separated by
.BR / ,
for example
.BR Ether()/IP()/TCP() .
Every layer has named fields with sensible defaults, and lengths and
checksums are computed automatically.
The result can be displayed, hex\-dumped, dissected, or appended to a pcapng
file that Wireshark, tshark and tcpdump read directly.
.PP
.B pcapsh
runs in three modes:
.TP
.B Interactive
With no script argument it starts a REPL with line editing, Tab completion
(protocol names, functions and variables) and history saved to
.I .pcapsh_history
in the current directory.
A
.B for
loop body is ended by an empty line.
Leave with
.BR exit() ,
.B quit()
or Ctrl\-D.
.TP
.B Script
With a file argument, each line is evaluated in order with no banner or
prompt.
Lines starting with
.B #
are comments, so a script may begin with a
.B #!
line and be made executable.
.TP
.B One\-shot
.B \-e
.I expression
evaluates a single expression and exits; handy from other shell scripts.
.PP
Beyond the native layers (Ether, IP, TCP, UDP, ICMP, Raw, DNS, TLS) more than a
hundred protocols are described in the
.B posa
language and loaded at startup; new ones can be added in a file or inline in
a script without recompiling.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-proto " \fIfile.posa\fR"
Load protocol definitions from
.IR file.posa .
May be repeated.
Any argument ending in
.B .posa
is loaded the same way without the flag.
.TP
.BI \-e " expression"
Evaluate
.I expression
and exit.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIfile\fR"
Redirect every
.B wrpcap()
in the script to
.IR file ,
whatever file name the script itself uses.
Lets one script produce differently named captures.
.TP
.B \-s
After the run, print the TLS session keys (NSS key log format) to standard
error.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keylog " \fIfile\fR"
Load an NSS TLS key log so that
.B rdpcap()
can decrypt TLS traffic.
.B $SSLKEYLOGFILE
is read automatically when set.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH QUICK START
.nf
.RS 4
$ pcapsh
>>> IP(dst="8.8.8.8")/UDP(dport=53)/DNS(rd=1,qd=DNSQR(qname="example.com"))
>>> hexdump(Ether()/IP()/TCP(dport=443,flags="S"))
>>> ls()                      # every known protocol
>>> ls(TCP)                   # fields of TCP, with defaults
>>> wrpcap("ping.pcapng", Ether()/IP()/ICMP())
>>> rdpcap("ping.pcapng")      # tshark\-like listing
>>> help()
.RE
.fi
.PP
The same lines saved in a file run unattended:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
$ pcapsh ping.pcapsh && tshark \-r ping.pcapng
.RE
.fi
.SH LANGUAGE
.SS Building packets
A layer is written
.IB Name ( field = value ", ...)" ,
and layers are stacked with
.BR / .
Fields not mentioned keep their default; fields such as IP
.BR len ,
.BR proto ,
.BR chksum ,
UDP
.BR len ,
and Ether
.B type
are filled in from the layers that follow unless set explicitly.
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
IP()
Ether(src="aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff")/IP(src="10.0.0.1",dst="10.0.0.2",ttl=1)/TCP(dport=22)
IP()/TCP(flags="PA")/"GET / HTTP/1.0\er\en\er\en"
IP(proto=47)/GRE(proto=IPV4)/IP(dst="192.168.2.1")/TCP()
.RE
.fi
.PP
Typing an expression at the prompt prints the packet layer by layer.
A layer of a protocol defined in
.B posa
may also be used alone, e.g.
.BR hexdump(SMB2(command=READ)) ,
which serialises only that header.
.SS Values
.TP
.B Numbers
Decimal
.RB ( 80 )
or hexadecimal
.RB ( 0x1F90 ).
Numeric fields accept
.BR + ,
.B \-
and
.B *
with the usual precedence, e.g.
.B sport=49000+$i
or
.BR seq=1+$i*68 .
Division is not available because
.B /
stacks layers.
.TP
.B Strings
Double\-quoted, with escapes
.BR \er ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \e\e ,
and
.BI \ex NN
for any byte.
A string after
.B /
becomes a Raw payload.
.TP
.B Addresses
IPv4 and MAC addresses are given as strings:
.B dst="10.0.0.1"
and
.BR src="02:00:0a:00:00:01" .
.TP
.B TCP flags
A string of letters
.RB ( F
FIN,
.B S
SYN,
.B R
RST,
.B P
PSH,
.B A
ACK,
.B U
URG), e.g.
.BR flags="SA" ,
or a number such as
.BR flags=0x12 .
.TP
.B Enum names
Fields with named values take the name directly:
.BR ARP(op=REPLY) ,
.BR SMB2(command=READ) ,
.BR DNSQR(qtype=MX) .
.B ls(Proto)
shows the names.
.SS Variables
.B name = expression
stores a packet, raw bytes, a TCP session or a number:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
req = IP(src="10.0.0.1",dst="10.0.0.2")/UDP(dport=123)/NTP()
hexdump(req)
wrpcap("ntp.pcapng", req)
.RE
.fi
.SS Comments
Everything from
.B #
to the end of a line is ignored.
.SH SCRIPTS
A script is a plain text file, by convention ending in
.BR .pcapsh ,
holding one statement per line.
.B wrpcap()
appends to an existing file, so delete old output before a re\-run if you
want a fresh capture:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
#!/usr/bin/env pcapsh
# ping.pcapsh \- two echo request/reply pairs
wrpcap("ping.pcapng", IP(src="10.0.0.1",dst="10.0.0.2")/ICMP(type=8,id=1,seq=1)/"hello")
wrpcap("ping.pcapng", IP(src="10.0.0.2",dst="10.0.0.1")/ICMP(type=0,id=1,seq=1)/"hello")
wrpcap("ping.pcapng", IP(src="10.0.0.1",dst="10.0.0.2")/ICMP(type=8,id=1,seq=2)/"hello")
wrpcap("ping.pcapng", IP(src="10.0.0.2",dst="10.0.0.1")/ICMP(type=0,id=1,seq=2)/"hello")
.RE
.fi
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
$ rm \-f ping.pcapng; pcapsh ping.pcapsh
$ pcapsh \-o other.pcapng ping.pcapsh     # same script, other file
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B exit()
or
.B quit()
in a script stops it immediately.
.SS Loops
.nf
.RS 4
for $\fIvar\fP in range(\fIstop\fP):
    \fIstatement\fP
    \fIstatement\fP
.RE
.fi
.PP
The body consists of the following lines indented by at least one space or
tab; it ends at the first unindented line (or, interactively, an empty line).
.TP
.BI range( n )
.I $var
takes
.BR 1 ", " 2 ", ... " \fIn\fP
(one\-based and inclusive, so it matches packet numbers).
.TP
.BI range( start ", " stop )
.IR start ", " start "+1, ... " stop "\-1."
.TP
.BI range( start ", " stop ", " step )
.IR start ", " start + step ", ... while below " stop .
.PP
The loop variable can be used in any numeric field or argument, including
arithmetic and
.BR frompcapng() ;
it is not substituted inside strings such as IP addresses.
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
# 20 NTP exchanges from ephemeral ports 49152\-49171
for $i in range(20):
    wrpcap("ntp.pcapng", IP(src="10.0.1.1",dst="10.0.0.2")/UDP(sport=49151+$i,dport=123)/NTP(li_vn_mode=CLIENT))
    wrpcap("ntp.pcapng", IP(src="10.0.0.2",dst="10.0.1.1")/UDP(sport=123,dport=49151+$i)/NTP(li_vn_mode=SERVER,stratum=1))
.RE
.fi
.SS Inline protocols
A protocol can be declared in the script itself and is usable at once:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
protocol MyHdr
    uint8  version defaults(1)
    uint8  flags   defaults(0)
        URGENT = 0x01
        RETRY  = 0x02
    uint16 length  defaults(0)
    uint32 session defaults(0)
end

wrpcap("custom.pcapng", IP()/UDP(dport=9000)/MyHdr(flags=URGENT,session=0xdeadbeef))
show("MyHdr", fromhex("02 01 00 08 de ad be ef"))
.RE
.fi
.PP
The field syntax is the same as in
.B .posa
files; see
.BR "DEFINING PROTOCOLS" .
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI hexdump( pkt )
Print a coloured hex and ASCII dump of the serialised packet.
.TP
.BI raw( pkt )
Print the bytes as an escaped string, as Scapy's
.B raw()
does.
.TP
.BR ls ([ \fIProto\fP ])
Without an argument, list every known protocol.
With one, list its fields, types, defaults and enum names.
.TP
.BI wrpcap(" file ", " pkt )
Write
.I pkt
to the pcapng
.IR file .
Creates the file (with section and interface headers) if it does not exist,
otherwise appends one Enhanced Packet Block.
The link type is always Ethernet; a packet with no
.B Ether
layer gets a default one prepended.
.TP
.BR rdpcap( \fIfile\fP [, \fIfilter\fP ])
List the packets of a pcap or pcapng file, one line each, like
.BR "tshark \-r" ,
optionally keeping only those matching a display
.I filter
(see
.BR "DISPLAY FILTERS" ).
Returns the number of matching packets.
.TP
.BI frompcapng(" file ", " n )
Return packet
.I n
(counting from 1) of a pcapng file as raw bytes, for
.BR show() ,
.BR hexdump() ,
.B raw()
or
.BR wrpcap() .
.B packet_number=\fIn\fP
is accepted as well.
.TP
.BI replacepkt(" file ", " n ", " pkt )
Replace packet
.I n
of an existing pcapng file in place.
.TP
.BI fromhex(" hex ")
Turn hex text into raw bytes.
Accepts a plain stream
.RB ( """00010100""" ),
space\-separated bytes
.RB ( """00 01 01 00""" ),
or a multi\-line Wireshark "Copy as Hex Dump" with offsets and ASCII column.
.TP
.BI show(" stack ", " bytes )
Dissect
.I bytes
through the
.BR / \-separated
layer names in
.IR stack ,
e.g.
.BR """Ether/IP/UDP/DNS""" ,
printing each layer and consuming its header before the next.
IP and TCP use their header\-length fields, so options are skipped.
Any posa protocol may appear in the stack, and a group name declared as
.BI Object< Group >
dispatches to the member whose fields match (e.g.
.B """IP/UDP/TFTP"""
picks TFTP_RRQ, TFTP_DATA, ... from the opcode).
.TP
.BI load(" file.posa ")
Load protocol definitions at run time.
.TP
.B RandShort()
A random 16\-bit number, e.g. for DNS transaction ids.
.TP
.BR help() ", " exit() ", " quit()
Show built\-in help; leave the shell.
.SH PROTOCOLS
.SS Native layers
.TP
.B Ether
.BR dst " (ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff), " src ", " type
(0x0800 by default; set
.B type=0x0806
for ARP).
.TP
.B IP
.BR src ", " dst " (127.0.0.1), " ttl " (64), " id ", " flags ", " frag ", " tos ,
.BR proto ", " len ", " chksum " (automatic)."
.TP
.B TCP
.BR sport " (20), " dport " (80), " seq ", " ack ", " flags " (""S""), " window ,
.BR dataofs ", " urgptr ", " chksum .
.TP
.B UDP
.BR sport ", " dport " (53), " len ", " chksum .
.TP
.B ICMP
.BR type " (8, echo request), " code ", " id ", " seq ", " chksum .
.TP
.B Raw
.BR load= \(dq...\(dq;
a bare string after
.B /
is the same thing.
.TP
.B DNS
.BR id ", " qr ", " opcode ", " aa ", " tc ", " rd ", " ra ", " rcode
(or the whole
.B flags
word),
.BR qdcount ", " ancount ", " nscount ", " arcount ,
and the sections
.BR qd= DNSQR(...),
.BR an= ", " ns= ", " ar= DNSRR(...).
Setting
.B qd
increments
.B qdcount
automatically.
.TP
.BR DNSQR( qname ", " qtype ", " qclass )
Question; types
.BR A ", " NS ", " CNAME ", " SOA ", " PTR ", " MX ", " AAAA ", " ANY .
.TP
.BR DNSRR( rrname ", " type ", " rclass ", " ttl ", " rdata )
Resource record;
.B rdata
is an IPv4 address for A records and a domain name for CNAME, NS, MX, PTR.
.SS posa protocols
Loaded at startup from the protocol directory (see
.BR FILES )
and from
.IR ~/.pcapsh_protos.posa .
They include, among many others, ARP, NTP, DHCP, GRE, VXLAN, RADIUS, SYSLOG,
NBT, NBNS, SMB2, DCERPC, LDAP, KRB5, HTTP, SIP, MQTT, Modbus, BGP, OSPF,
QUIC, WireGuard, SNMP, TFTP and Telnet.
Run
.B ls()
for the complete list and
.BI ls( Proto )
for fields.
.SS TLS and SSH helpers
These return ready\-made records to put after a TCP layer.
TLS uses TLS 1.2 with the NULL cipher, so Wireshark decodes the inner
protocol without keys.
.TP
.B TLS_CLIENT_HELLO() TLS_SERVER_HELLO() TLS_CERTIFICATE()
.TQ
.B TLS_CHANGE_CIPHER_SPEC() TLS_FINISHED()
Handshake records (50, 47, 12, 6 and 21 bytes long).
.TP
.B TLS()
Wraps whatever follows into an Application Data record:
.BR TCP(flags="PA")/TLS()/LDAP(op_tag=BIND_REQUEST) .
.TP
.BR SSH_KEXINIT ([ side="server" ]) ", " SSH_NEWKEYS()
SSH key exchange messages; send the version banner as a plain string,
.BR """SSH\-2.0\-OpenSSH_9.5\er\en""" .
.SH TCP SESSIONS
Writing a coherent TCP conversation by hand means tracking sequence and
acknowledgement numbers.
A
.B TCPSession
does it for you: each helper returns a complete Ether/IP/TCP packet and
advances the session state, and
.B wrpcap()
just writes it.
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
s = TCPSession("\fIclient_ip\fP", "\fIserver_ip\fP", \fIclient_port\fP, \fIserver_port\fP)
.RE
.fi
.PP
MAC addresses are derived from the IPs (02:00 followed by the four octets).
.PP
.TS
l l l.
Function	Direction	Flags
_
syn(s)	client \(-> server	S
syn_ack(s)	server \(-> client	SA
tcp_ack(s), ack(s)	client \(-> server	A
client_send(s, "data")	client \(-> server	PA
server_send(s, "data")	server \(-> client	PA
client_fin(s)	client \(-> server	FA
server_fin_ack(s)	server \(-> client	FA
.TE
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
# http.pcapsh \- a complete HTTP/1.0 exchange
s = TCPSession("192.168.1.100", "93.184.216.34", 54321, 80)
wrpcap("http.pcapng", syn(s))
wrpcap("http.pcapng", syn_ack(s))
wrpcap("http.pcapng", ack(s))             # same as tcp_ack(s)
wrpcap("http.pcapng", client_send(s, "GET / HTTP/1.0\er\enHost: example.com\er\en\er\en"))
wrpcap("http.pcapng", server_send(s, "HTTP/1.0 200 OK\er\enContent\-Length: 5\er\en\er\enhello"))
wrpcap("http.pcapng", client_fin(s))
wrpcap("http.pcapng", server_fin_ack(s))
.RE
.fi
.PP
Several sessions can be interleaved in one file; packets appear in the order
they are written.
.SH DISPLAY FILTERS
.B rdpcap()
accepts a subset of Wireshark display filter syntax:
.TP
.B Existence
.BR tcp ", " dns ", " dns.qry.name
.TP
.B Comparison
.BR "== != > < >= <=" " (or " "eq ne gt lt ge le" ),
.BR contains ,
.B matches
(POSIX extended regex)
.TP
.B Logic
.BR "&& || !" " (or " "and or not" "), parentheses"
.TP
.B Values
numbers, "strings", IPv4 addresses with optional /CIDR, MAC addresses
.TP
.B Slices and sets
.BR "eth.src[0:3] == 00:00:5e" ,
.BR "tcp.port in {80, 443}" ,
.B udp.port in {1..1024}
.TP
.B Aliases
.BR ip.addr ", " tcp.port ", " udp.port ", " eth.addr
match either direction.
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
rdpcap("capture.pcapng", "ip.addr == 10.0.0.0/8 && tcp.port in {80,443}")
rdpcap("capture.pcapng", "dns && !(ip.src == 8.8.8.8)")
.RE
.fi
.SH DEFINING PROTOCOLS
New protocols are described in
.B posa
files, loaded with
.BR \-p ,
.BR load() ,
by naming them on the command line, or by putting them in
.IR ~/.pcapsh_protos.posa .
They appear in
.BR ls() ,
can be built like any layer, and can be dissected with
.BR show() .
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
Object<main> MyTLV
    uint8  type   "Type"   defaults(1)
        DATA      = 1
        CONTROL   = 2
        KEEPALIVE = 3
    uint8  flags  defaults(0)
    uint16 length defaults(0)
    uint32 seq    defaults(0)
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B defaults(...)
gives the value used when the field is not set: a number, a quoted string or
byte string, or an address written as is
.RB ( defaults(10.0.0.1) ).
Indented
.IB NAME " = " value
lines under a field declare its enum names.
The optional quoted label is used for display.
.PP
Field types:
.TP
.B uint8 uint16 uint32 uint64
Big\-endian integers.
.TP
.B le_uint16 le_uint32 le_uint64
Little\-endian integers (Windows protocols such as SMB2 and DCERPC).
.TP
.BI enum< uint8 | uint16 | uint32 >
Integer with named values.
.TP
.BR mac ", " ip4
Six\- and four\-byte addresses.
.TP
.BI bytes< N > ", " str< N >
Fixed width, zero padded.
.TP
.B cstring
NUL\-terminated text.
.TP
.BI "string " name " until """ delim """"
Text up to and including a delimiter, e.g.
.BR "until ""\er\en""" .
.PP
Declaring several objects as
.BI Object< Group >
lets
.BI show(""... /Group "", " data )
choose the right one automatically.
See
.I doc/posa.md
in the libpcapng sources for the full language.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS DNS query and answer
.nf
.RS 4
wrpcap("dns.pcapng", IP(src="192.168.1.10",dst="8.8.8.8")/UDP(sport=54321,dport=53)/DNS(id=0x1111,rd=1,qd=DNSQR(qname="example.com")))
wrpcap("dns.pcapng", IP(src="8.8.8.8",dst="192.168.1.10")/UDP(sport=53,dport=54321)/DNS(id=0x1111,qr=1,rd=1,ra=1,qd=DNSQR(qname="example.com"),an=DNSRR(rrname="example.com",type=A,ttl=300,rdata="93.184.216.34"),ancount=1))
.RE
.fi
.SS ARP request and reply
.nf
.RS 4
wrpcap("arp.pcapng", Ether(type=0x0806)/ARP(op=REQUEST,spa="10.0.0.100",tpa="10.0.0.1"))
wrpcap("arp.pcapng", Ether(type=0x0806)/ARP(op=REPLY,sha="aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:01",spa="10.0.0.1",tha="02:00:0a:00:00:64",tpa="10.0.0.100"))
.RE
.fi
.SS Port sweep with a loop
.nf
.RS 4
for $p in range(20, 30):
    wrpcap("sweep.pcapng", IP(src="10.0.0.5",dst="10.0.0.1")/TCP(sport=40000+$p,dport=$p,flags="S"))
.RE
.fi
.SS DHCP discover / offer
.nf
.RS 4
wrpcap("dhcp.pcapng", Ether(src="aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff")/IP(src="0.0.0.0",dst="255.255.255.255")/UDP(sport=68,dport=67)/DHCP(op=BOOTREQUEST,xid=0xdeadbeef))
wrpcap("dhcp.pcapng", Ether(src="00:11:22:33:44:55",dst="aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff")/IP(src="192.168.1.1",dst="255.255.255.255")/UDP(sport=67,dport=68)/DHCP(op=BOOTREPLY,xid=0xdeadbeef,yiaddr="192.168.1.100"))
.RE
.fi
.SS Tunnels
.nf
.RS 4
wrpcap("gre.pcapng", IP(src="10.0.0.1",dst="10.0.0.2",proto=47)/GRE(proto=IPV4)/IP(src="192.168.1.1",dst="192.168.2.1")/TCP(flags="S"))
wrpcap("vxlan.pcapng", IP(src="10.0.0.1",dst="10.0.0.2")/UDP(dport=4789)/VXLAN()/Ether()/IP()/TCP(dport=8080))
.RE
.fi
.SS RFC 3164 syslog
.B SYSLOG()
emits only the PRI byte; for a full message use a string
(PRI = facility * 8 + severity):
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
wrpcap("syslog.pcapng", IP(src="10.0.0.1",dst="10.0.0.254")/UDP(sport=514,dport=514)/"<38>May 11 10:00:01 dc01 sshd[1234]: Accepted publickey for admin")
.RE
.fi
.SS LDAP over TLS
.nf
.RS 4
s = TCPSession("10.0.1.1", "10.0.0.1", 50000, 636)
wrpcap("ldaps.pcapng", syn(s))
wrpcap("ldaps.pcapng", syn_ack(s))
wrpcap("ldaps.pcapng", tcp_ack(s))
wrpcap("ldaps.pcapng", Ether()/IP(src="10.0.1.1",dst="10.0.0.1")/TCP(sport=50000,dport=636,flags="PA")/TLS_CLIENT_HELLO())
wrpcap("ldaps.pcapng", Ether()/IP(src="10.0.1.1",dst="10.0.0.1")/TCP(sport=50000,dport=636,flags="PA")/TLS()/LDAP(op_tag=BIND_REQUEST,message_id=1))
.RE
.fi
.PP
(For a capture that Wireshark fully reassembles, set
.B seq
and
.B ack
on each TCP layer: each TLS record advances them by its length.)
.SS Dissect bytes copied from Wireshark
.nf
.RS 4
d = fromhex("0000   45 00 00 34 00 01 00 00  40 11 f6 c4 c0 a8 01 05
0010   08 08 08 08 c3 a8 00 35  00 20 41 a1 12 34 01 00
0020   00 01 00 00 00 00 00 00")
show("IP/UDP/DNS", d)
.RE
.fi
.SS Inspect and edit an existing capture
.nf
.RS 4
rdpcap("capture.pcapng", "udp.dstport == 53")
for $n in range(3):
    show("Ether/IP/TCP", frompcapng("capture.pcapng", $n))

replacepkt("capture.pcapng", 2, Ether()/IP(dst="10.0.0.99")/TCP(dport=8080,flags="S"))
.RE
.fi
.SS From the shell
.nf
.RS 4
$ pcapsh \-e 'hexdump(IP()/UDP(dport=53)/DNS(rd=1,qd=DNSQR(qname="a.example")))'
$ pcapsh \-e 'ls(SMB2)'
